So the API root returns JSON. The /health endpoint might not exist. The proxy for /hermes/api/ is rewriting to /, but maybe the rewrite is not working correctly. Let's check: we used rewrite ^/hermes/api/(.*)$ /$1 break; For request /hermes/api/health, the rewrite would capture "health" and rewrite to /health. That should work. But why 500? Possibly because the API does not have /health. Let's test /hermes/api/ (root) without rewrites: maybe we should just proxy_pass to http://127.0.0.1:8000/ without rewriting, so that /hermes/api/ becomes /hermes/api/ on the API, which is not correct.

Better to not rewrite and instead change the API to expect the prefix. But we can adjust the proxy to strip the prefix by using proxy_pass with URI: proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000/; This will replace the matched location path with /. Since location is /hermes/api/, and proxy_pass has /, then /hermes/api/foo becomes /foo. That's what we want.

So we can remove the rewrite and just use proxy_pass with trailing slash.

Let's change the location to:

location /hermes/api/ {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000/;
    ... other headers...
}

That should work.

